{
  "image_name": "0d64de540a8c85d909486cab38f8e796.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a metatranscriptomics workflow and a ribosome-profiling method used to analyze gene expression in mixed microbial communities. On the left, sequencing reads are processed through several alternative routes depending on available reference information. Reads can first be used to identify which species are present from DNA markers, then mapped to pangenomes, with remaining unmapped reads aligned to protein databases. Another branch maps reads to metagenome-assembled genomes or directly to reference genomes. A third branch assembles the transcriptome de novo, then performs gene calling and annotation before mapping or aligning sequences. All routes converge on sample-by-feature matrices that quantify genes, pathways, and taxa across samples.\n\nOn the right, the panel labeled MetaRibo-Seq shows how ribosome-bound RNA is isolated to capture actively translated transcripts. Ribosomes are stalled with chloramphenicol, structured RNA and translating mRNA are digested with MNase, and then protein is removed while protected RNA fragments are collected. The overall message is a comparison between computational strategies for assigning metatranscriptomic reads and an experimental approach that enriches for translation-level information, linking community composition to functional activity.",
